What are your critiques of the given DT implementations, as
specified in Step 2.5?

-There is an Inconsistent parameter order in nodeDT.h. For instance,
Node_new(oPPath, oNParent, ...) has the path first.
Node_hasChild(oNParent, oPPath, ...) has the parent first. 
-There are inconsistent return conventions in nodeDT.h.
Node_getChild returns an int for the status and sets *poNResult to
be the child node of oNParent with identifier ulChildID, if one exists,
but Node_getParent and Node_getPath return the nodes directly.
-Node_toString is never actually called by dt.c since DT_toString
builds its string itself. This is unnecessary and does not need to
be in nodeDT.h.
-An important concept of modularity is weak coupling. nodeDT.c and
checkerDT.h depend on each other, which does not obey this rule
-In Node_new, there are a lot of ways the process could fail, and
there is the same freeing process for each one. Instead of repeating
the text several times, we could just create a helper function to
free and call it once creating the node goes wrong.
-This would probably help with ensuring memory is properly freed.
For instance, when Node_addChild fails, we don’t free the space
allocated for children of the new node. This could have probably
been prevented with the modification above.
-DT_traversePath uses a “magic number” to indicate the node numbers.
For example, there could be a constant variable to indicate
ROOT_NODE = 1, instead of using just the number (line 68). It would
be better for understanding the code and correcting it.
-Poor naming for the parameters of DT_preOrderTraversal (line 336).
One letter names are not good indicators of what the variables
stand for - not good for readability.


